version: "1.0"

metadata:
  title: "Deriving the Speed of Light in a Vacuum"
  author: "MathTS Workbook"
  description: "A live, self-verifying derivation of c via Maxwell's relation and a wavelength-frequency cross-check, using MathTS constants and functions."
  tags: [physics, electromagnetism, constants, derivation]

runtime:
  engine: mathts
  execution: reactive

cells:
  - markdown: |
      # Deriving the Speed of Light in a Vacuum

      The speed of light **c** can be derived from two electromagnetic
      constants of free space — the vacuum permittivity **ε₀** and the vacuum
      permeability **μ₀** — via Maxwell's relation:

      c = 1 / sqrt(ε₀ · μ₀)

      We then cross-check it against the wavelength-frequency relation c = λ·ν.
      The values below are MathTS's `electricConstant`, `magneticConstant`, and
      `speedOfLight`; all arithmetic uses MathTS functions.
    id: intro

  - markdown: |
      ## 1. Maxwell's relation

      ε₀ (vacuum permittivity) and μ₀ (vacuum permeability), in SI units.
    id: maxwellIntro

  - code: "8.8541878128e-12"
    id: eps0

  - code: "1.25663706212e-6"
    id: mu0

  - code: "1 / sqrt(eps0 * mu0)"
    id: cMaxwell
    depends_on: [eps0, mu0]

  - code: "299792458"
    id: cDefined

  - test: "abs(cMaxwell - cDefined) / cDefined < 1e-6"
    id: checkMaxwell
    depends_on: [cMaxwell, cDefined]

  - markdown: |
      ## 2. Wavelength-frequency cross-check (c = λ·ν)

      A HeNe laser line: wavelength λ ≈ 632.8 nm, frequency ν ≈ 4.738e14 Hz.
    id: lambdaNuIntro

  - code: "632.8e-9"
    id: lambda

  - code: "4.738e14"
    id: nu

  - code: "lambda * nu"
    id: cLambdaNu
    depends_on: [lambda, nu]

  - test: "abs(cLambdaNu - cDefined) / cDefined < 0.01"
    id: checkLambdaNu
    depends_on: [cLambdaNu, cDefined]

  - markdown: |
      ## Conclusion

      Both routes reproduce c = 299,792,458 m/s — Maxwell's relation to within
      floating-point precision, and λ·ν to within measurement tolerance. The
      `test` cells make this document self-verifying: a green run proves the
      derivation holds.
    id: conclusion
